;; The files of the token check (bin/repr-c check --tokens; doc/C23-SURVEY.md, "As built: C2") whose
;; dumps are known to differ from cc's, by level and corpus (CS40 (a)): at :pp each with its class
;; and :gcc, the SHA-256 of gcc -E -P's tokens (one a line, no separators), which the port's stream
;; must give: the reference is the judge where cc is wrong; at :lex each with its class; at :gcc (CS51
;; (a)) each file whose stream is not gcc -E's (test/repr/c/pp-gcc.sha256) with its class. Written by
;; bin/repr-c check --tokens --record, the classes kept. Read with the Clojure reader.
{:classes
 {:assembler "an assembler source (.S) gcc preprocesses as assembler-with-cpp, its lexing not C's"
  :c23-colons "C23's :: punctuator (6.4.6), which cc's scanner lacks: a paste of : and : is one token to gcc in C23, two to the port (whose lexer is cc's), the tokens' texts the same"
  :c23-if-true "true and false in a C23 #if (6.10.1): 1 and 0, where cc reads identifiers as 0"
  :cc-panic "cc's preprocessor panics (the port's does too, at the same place), where gcc reads the file"
  :digit-separators "C23's digit separators (6.4.4.1) in a #line's number, which cc's ParseUint refuses (the directive ignored)"
  :elifdef "C23's #elifdef and #elifndef (6.10.2), which cc reads as non-directives inside a group"
  :embed "C23's #embed and __has_embed (6.10.4, 6.10.1), which cc lacks: cc reads #embed as a non-directive and __has_embed as an identifier; the port lexes #embed's header name and expands the resource's bytes, its parameters as gcc's (limit, prefix, suffix, if_empty, gnu::offset, gnu::base64)"
  :error-test "a file the reference rejects (gcc -E's status not 0, a dg-error test): the port reports an error too"
  :eval-depth "the preprocessor recurses deeper than the evaluator's 10,000 interpreted calls (CS60)"
  :gnu-builtin-macro "gcc's __BASE_FILE__, __INCLUDE_LEVEL__, __FILE_NAME__, __TIMESTAMP__"
  :has-c-attribute "__has_c_attribute (6.10.1), which cc answers 0 (an identifier): the port answers gcc's recorded values"
  :has-gnu "gcc's __has_attribute, __has_builtin, __has_feature and __has_extension: cc answers its own (0, or its prefix rules); the port gcc's recorded answers"
  :invalid-paste "a ## that does not make one token (undefined, 6.10.5.3): cc joins the texts, gcc (and the port) keep the two tokens and report the error"
  :pragma-once "#pragma once, which cc ignores"
  :trigraphs "phase 1's trigraphs (5.2.1.1), which cc does not read: gcc reads them at -std=c89 to c17 and with -trigraphs, and the port does likewise (CS50 (a))"
  :u-prefix "C11's u'x', U'x', u\"x\", U\"x\" (6.4.4.5, 6.4.5): cc's scanner reads the prefix as an identifier; the port one token (of the wide kinds)"
  :ucn-identifier "universal character names in identifiers and pp-numbers (6.4.3), which cc's scanner reads as a backslash and an identifier: one token to gcc and the port (CS50 (a))"
  :undiagnosed "a file gcc rejects (a dg-error test) whose error the port's preprocessor does not diagnose (UCNs not valid in C23, __VA_OPT__ misuse): C7's error diagnostics"
  :xid-identifier "an identifier holding a character from 0x80 that is not a letter (a combining mark): one identifier to gcc (C23's XID set), two tokens to cc and the port (CS63)"
  :unclassified "a difference no rule names yet"
  :va-opt "C23's __VA_OPT__ (6.10.5.1), which cc lacks: cc leaves it an identifier; the port substitutes it as gcc (the variable arguments' presence after expansion, placemarkers where its body is pasted)"}
 :pp
